                <pb facs="0326" n="320" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg320"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115/621: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-04-24T21:00:00+09:00">April 24, 1939—9
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 24—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>197. Department’s 107, April 22, 1 p.m. I took occasion at a luncheon today to
                    mention this matter to Sawada for conveyance to Arita and also to Yoshizawa.
                    Yoshizawa said that a considerable number of staff officers of the Japanese
                    forces in China were recently in Tokyo for consultation and that in the
                    attendant conferences there had been “some very plain speaking” to the effect
                    that the bombing of American property in China must cease He said that these
                    facts would be set forth in the Japanese reply which is now being drafted to our
                    recent note on that subject.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
